{"kind":"request","text":"When an agent promises work on an observed issue, render that promise as the GitHub assignee. Hugh's framing: a promise in the workroom should show up on the issue, so someone reading GitHub can see the work is claimed without being told.\n\nThis is rendering rather than a command. It projects durable state — who holds the live promise — onto a field the connector owns and nothing reads back, and it is naturally idempotent: re-rendering the same promise sets the same assignee. So it needs no ceremony of its own, unlike opening a pull request, which is a discrete side effect and goes through the work loop.\n\nTwo problems have to be settled first, and neither is incidental.\n\nThere is no mapping from a workroom actor to a GitHub account, and inventing one silently would be the dishonesty this connector exists to avoid. I checked: no actor in the roster carries a GitHub login — claude, codex, hugh and github-connector all carry name, kind, roles and membership only. Assigning the token's owner as a stand-in would be the worst available answer, because the issue would then say Hugh is working on something Claude promised, and the GitHub side would be asserting an attribution the log contradicts. The mapping has to be stated by someone with the authority to state it, and the charter is the natural place, since it already names the connector, owner, repository and actor and is already ratified. An actor with no mapping simply renders no assignee.\n\nGitHub silently drops assignees it does not accept. The issues API accepts an `assignees` array, and usernames without push access to the repository are discarded without error — the call returns success and the issue ends up assigned to nobody. That is exactly the failure this workroom has spent the day chasing in its own log: an operation that reports success and means nothing. So the connector must read back what GitHub actually recorded and treat a dropped assignee as a failure rather than trusting the status code. A rendering that cannot confirm itself should say so rather than appear to have worked.\n\nWorth deciding while implementing: what happens when the promise is superseded or the commitment is reneged. The honest rendering is to clear the assignee, since the field is a projection of current state rather than a history, and the log remains the record of who once promised.","body":{"conditions":"A live promise against an observed issue renders as that issue's GitHub assignee, and the rendering is idempotent so a repeated run makes no further change; the workroom-actor-to-GitHub-login mapping is stated durably by an actor with authority rather than inferred, with the charter the default home for it, and an actor with no mapping renders no assignee rather than falling back to the token's owner; the connector reads back what GitHub recorded and treats a silently dropped assignee as a failure, demonstrated by a test in which the API accepts the call and returns an issue whose assignees do not contain the requested login; a promise that is superseded or reneged clears the assignee, or the chosen alternative is recorded with its reason; the connector still writes nothing without a live charter naming this connector, repository and actor; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge; sequenced after the inbound half reaches main, since the outbound half builds on it.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}